    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(7))  # F0..F6\n    customers = list(range(8))  # C0..C7\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(90, 63), (23, 45), (15, 21), (38, 58), (6, 59), (10, 25), (19, 43)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(34, 21), (53, 81), (1, 62), (42, 56), (16, 57), (2, 90), (40, 77), (32, 87)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [124.0, 95.0, 145.0, 197.0, 65.0, 55.0, 148.0]\n    customer_demands = [43.0, 12.0, 48.0, 43.0, 20.0, 38.0, 50.0, 29.0]\n    fixed_costs = [213.0, 338.0, 251.0, 395.0, 217.0, 215.0, 464.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse location decision and distribution plan\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at the point where we need to decide which warehouses to open and how to route products to our customers in a way that keeps total cost down while meeting all volume requirements. We have a defined list of candidate warehouse locations, captured as map coordinates in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (90, 63)\n  - F1: (23, 45)\n  - F2: (15, 21)\n  - F3: (38, 58)\n  - F4: (6, 59)\n  - F5: (10, 25)\n  - F6: (19, 43), and our customer destinations are provided as map coordinates in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (34, 21)\n  - C1: (53, 81)\n  - C2: (1, 62)\n  - C3: (42, 56)\n  - C4: (16, 57)\n  - C5: (2, 90)\n  - C6: (40, 77)\n  - C7: (32, 87). For clarity, distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates.\n\nOpening any warehouse comes with a fixed cost outlined in [213.0, 338.0, 251.0, 395.0, 217.0, 215.0, 464.0]. Once a warehouse is open, shipping spend will depend on how far we ship and how much we ship, using a transport rate of 0.5. Each warehouse can only handle up to its capacity, which is detailed in [124.0, 95.0, 145.0, 197.0, 65.0, 55.0, 148.0], and each customer\u2019s required demand must be fully supplied, as listed in [43.0, 12.0, 48.0, 43.0, 20.0, 38.0, 50.0, 29.0]. A warehouse can only ship if it is opened.\n\nThe decision in front of us is twofold: which warehouses to open and how to allocate shipments from those open warehouses to each customer. The goal is straightforward\u2014minimize the total cost of the network by balancing the upfront cost to open facilities with the ongoing cost to move product, all while respecting capacity limits and meeting every customer\u2019s full demand.\n\nIn practical terms, this means weighing trade-offs such as whether it\u2019s more economical to open an additional, closer warehouse to cut delivery distances, or to serve customers from a smaller set of locations with lower opening costs but potentially longer hauls. The straight-line distance measure gives us a consistent way to estimate transportation cost across all facility\u2013customer pairs.\n\nOnce we validate these inputs and any service preferences, we will run the analysis and come back with a recommended set of warehouses to open, the shipment plan from each open location to each customer, and the expected total cost for the chosen configuration.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse location decision and distribution plan\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at the point where we need to decide which warehouses to open and how to route products to our customers in a way that keeps total cost down while meeting all volume requirements. We have a defined list of candidate warehouse locations, captured as map coordinates in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and our customer destinations are provided as map coordinates in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. For clarity, distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates.\n\nOpening any warehouse comes with a fixed cost outlined in {FIXED_COSTS}. Once a warehouse is open, shipping spend will depend on how far we ship and how much we ship, using a transport rate of {TRANSPORT_RATE}. Each warehouse can only handle up to its capacity, which is detailed in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, and each customer\u2019s required demand must be fully supplied, as listed in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. A warehouse can only ship if it is opened.\n\nThe decision in front of us is twofold: which warehouses to open and how to allocate shipments from those open warehouses to each customer. The goal is straightforward\u2014minimize the total cost of the network by balancing the upfront cost to open facilities with the ongoing cost to move product, all while respecting capacity limits and meeting every customer\u2019s full demand.\n\nIn practical terms, this means weighing trade-offs such as whether it\u2019s more economical to open an additional, closer warehouse to cut delivery distances, or to serve customers from a smaller set of locations with lower opening costs but potentially longer hauls. The straight-line distance measure gives us a consistent way to estimate transportation cost across all facility\u2013customer pairs.\n\nOnce we validate these inputs and any service preferences, we will run the analysis and come back with a recommended set of warehouses to open, the shipment plan from each open location to each customer, and the expected total cost for the chosen configuration."
